<div class="prose">
  <p class="section-label">About</p>
  <h1>I'm Phil Venice.</h1>
  <p>My background is in supply chain risk management, third-party risk, and enterprise
    program leadership. I spent 11 years at Boeing and now work in product management at LMI.</p>
  <p>The question I keep coming back to is practical: given what we know about a supply
    chain, what should we actually change?</p>
  <h2>Risk reduction is a systems problem</h2>
  <p>Engineering requirements, supplier capabilities, commercial incentives, and recovery
    timelines shape the choices available to a business. Looking at any one of them in
    isolation can leave the decisive constraint out of view.</p>
  <p>I’m especially interested in decisions that are difficult to reverse. A specification
    can narrow the supply base long before a buyer places an order. Qualifying an alternative
    can take longer than the business can tolerate an interruption. Those consequences
    deserve attention while the design is still being decided.</p>
  <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
  <p>I write about moving from risk information to feasible interventions, preserving
    alternatives through design and qualification choices, and helping engineering,
    procurement, and operations make those tradeoffs together.</p>
  <p>The aim is to help organizations build the capability to make better supply chain
    decisions themselves. I develop these ideas through public sources, clearly labeled
    hypothetical examples, and original analysis.</p>
  <p>This is my personal writing. Views are my own.</p>
  <p><a href="/blog/">Read the essays</a> or <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>.</p>
</div>
